WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:01.548
- [Narrator] Airbnb will go public

00:00:01.548 --> 00:00:05.533
with an estimated market
valuation of over $42 billion.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.630
A remarkable feet when
considering just eight months ago,

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.020
the home-sharing giant was in crisis mode

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.700
with its business being crushed
by the Coronavirus pandemic.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.740
From April to June, 2020,
revenue was down 72%

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.800
as travel and bookings came to a virtual

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.665
standstill worldwide.

00:00:23.665 --> 00:00:27.590
Here's how Airbnb managed to
pivot towards profitability

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.400
during a global pandemic
and how it might fair

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.673
amongst continued travel
restrictions and beyond.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.340
When the pandemic struck in March,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.125
travel restrictions around the
world sent Airbnb's bookings

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:42.290
and revenues into free-fall.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.746
Bookings in Europe alone went down 80%.

00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:47.092
Airbnb was burning through its reserves

00:00:47.092 --> 00:00:50.290
and needed a swift infusion of cash.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:54.496
So its first lifeline was
securing $2 billion in loans,

00:00:54.496 --> 00:00:57.010
but these didn't come cheap.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.656
- So when the pandemic
hit, Airbnb was bleeding

00:00:59.656 --> 00:01:02.950
through its cash reserves and
it needed a swift infusion

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:03.910
of cash.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.036
But the loan that they got was,

00:01:06.036 --> 00:01:08.750
came at a very steep interest rate

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.870
and it was an interest rate of 11%,

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.070
which is often associated
with distressed assets.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.737
- [Narrator] At this
point, Airbnb's valuation

00:01:15.737 --> 00:01:19.009
had fallen from $31 billion in 2017

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:22.240
to 18 billion in April, 2020.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.820
Despite this, Airbnb
was still under pressure

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.640
to go public by year's end.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.567
- So Airbnb CEO had toyed
with the idea of going public

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:33.930
for years, but he'd stopped
short of pulling the trigger.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.790
Now that was leading to
some anxieties setting in

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.338
among early investors and
a handful of employees

00:01:41.338 --> 00:01:45.463
who were set to lose their
stock options next year.

00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:47.648
- [Narrator] Like many other
companies during the pandemic,

00:01:47.648 --> 00:01:51.517
Airbnb dramatically
reduced its cost to get by,

00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:55.476
costs that have grown significantly
over the last few years.

00:01:55.476 --> 00:01:57.946
Before the pandemic, Airbnb had expanded

00:01:57.946 --> 00:02:01.096
into new areas like
transportation and media

00:02:01.096 --> 00:02:03.550
and spent big on things like marketing

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.520
and a new corporate headquarters,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.320
increasing the company's total costs

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.870
more than five fold between 2015 and 2019.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.607
- So even before the pandemic,

00:02:12.607 --> 00:02:15.687
you had board members asking executives,

00:02:15.687 --> 00:02:18.860
"Hey, why are your costs so high?"

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.520
Airbnb spent big to expand
into all sorts of new areas.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.820
It also spent big on a
new corporate headquarters

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.860
and it typically spends heavily on things

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.360
like sales and marketing.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.890
- [Narrator] To alleviate
its balance sheets,

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.898
Airbnb cut big expenses, put
many non-corporate projects

00:02:36.898 --> 00:02:39.986
on hold and laid off a
quarter of its staff.

00:02:39.986 --> 00:02:43.620
- They early on said that their founders

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.850
wouldn't take a salary.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.630
They cut pay in half for top executives.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.046
They slashed sales and marketing expenses,

00:02:50.046 --> 00:02:52.900
but it still wasn't enough.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.337
We spoke to CEO, Brian Chesky,

00:02:55.337 --> 00:03:00.337
and he said he went through
his accounts line by line

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.270
to see what could be cut.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.900
- [Narrator] But none
of Airbnb's maneuvers

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.900
would work if people didn't start booking

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.346
on its platform again.

00:03:08.346 --> 00:03:10.956
So the third move it made
was to adapt its platform

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:12.860
for pandemic travel.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.030
- People were sitting at home.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.620
They still wanted to travel
but they couldn't get

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.515
onto a flight.

00:03:17.515 --> 00:03:21.100
So what people start doing
is that they start driving

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.030
to smaller towns and cities.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.550
And what do they do?

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.739
They book Airbnbs because big hotel chains

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.937
don't have a footprint there.

00:03:28.937 --> 00:03:31.128
So Airbnb gets lucky.

00:03:31.128 --> 00:03:32.590
- [Narrator] Before the pandemic,

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.476
big cities visited by tourists
had been Airbnb's strength,

00:03:35.476 --> 00:03:38.770
but these new local stays
in primarily suburban

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.377
and rural locations became
the company's focus.

00:03:41.377 --> 00:03:44.440
- CEO Brian Chesky sees this trend

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.990
and immediately capitalizes on it.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.347
- [Narrator] The company
redesigned its website and its app.

00:03:49.347 --> 00:03:51.608
So its algorithm would
show prospective travelers,

00:03:51.608 --> 00:03:54.640
everything from cabins
to lavish beach houses

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.140
near where they lived.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.720
By August more than half
of bookings were made

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.870
for stays within 300
miles of guests location,

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.510
according to the company.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.997
And these moves help stem
the losses in reservations.

00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:10.996
The company reported a surprise
profit in the third quarter.

00:04:10.996 --> 00:04:13.320
This quarter is typically
the most lucrative

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.487
for the company and
Airbnb has turned a profit

00:04:15.487 --> 00:04:19.170
in that period every year since 2017.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.350
Airbnb has never had a full year profit,

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.019
but it's turned around
during the global pandemic,

00:04:23.019 --> 00:04:26.680
and at a time when the
hotel industry is drowning,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.440
has gooey investors confidence

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.574
and helped its valuation bounce back.

00:04:30.574 --> 00:04:33.560
- One of Airbnb's near term challenges

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.805
is of course the pandemic.

00:04:35.805 --> 00:04:38.550
What will that look like in the future?

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.979
How does travel change
as a result of that?

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:44.631
Separately, the company
has traditionally struggled

00:04:44.631 --> 00:04:47.504
to police crime on its platform,

00:04:47.504 --> 00:04:52.340
and that is expected to
get a lot more scrutiny

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.900
as the company sells its
shares to the public.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.406
- [Narrator] Skeptics
wonder how long the company

00:04:57.406 --> 00:05:00.379
can keep costs down and
stick to its core business.

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:03.080
And questions remain on whether the growth

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.660
in revenue and bookings is sustainable.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.071
Or whether Airbnb is
simply the main beneficiary

00:05:08.071 --> 00:05:10.693
of a battered hospitality industry.

